WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.800
In one class, nine of the twenty-eight students

00:00:02.880 --> 00:00:05.320
who were counted have attached earlobes.

00:00:05.600 --> 00:00:07.840
Does that tell you how common attached earlobes

00:00:07.920 --> 00:00:09.360
are in everyone your age?

00:00:09.920 --> 00:00:10.760
Not yet.

00:00:11.040 --> 00:00:13.280
It depends on how the data were collected,

00:00:13.360 --> 00:00:15.920
how big the sample was, and how carefully

00:00:16.000 --> 00:00:17.800
each earlobe was judged.

00:00:18.160 --> 00:00:21.120
In this practical you'll collect, record and present

00:00:21.200 --> 00:00:24.080
frequency data, and learn to say exactly what

00:00:24.160 --> 00:00:26.160
it does and doesn't show.

00:00:27.367 --> 00:00:30.166
The aim: collect data on how common a

00:00:30.247 --> 00:00:33.766
characteristic is in a population, present it clearly,

00:00:33.807 --> 00:00:37.206
and identify trends, patterns and limitations.

00:00:37.527 --> 00:00:40.246
Choose a characteristic that's easy to see, quick

00:00:40.287 --> 00:00:43.606
to judge, and not sensitive, like earlobe shape

00:00:43.687 --> 00:00:44.646
or tongue rolling.

00:00:45.207 --> 00:00:47.926
Neither is a simple one-gene trait, so you're

00:00:48.047 --> 00:00:51.126
counting how common the characteristic is, not working

00:00:51.207 --> 00:00:52.646
out allele frequencies.

00:00:53.867 --> 00:00:56.466
Data about people needs their agreement.

00:00:56.747 --> 00:00:59.066
Get your teacher to approve the plan, tell

00:00:59.227 --> 00:01:02.106
everyone what's being collected and why, and let

00:01:02.187 --> 00:01:04.946
anyone say no without giving a reason.

00:01:05.227 --> 00:01:07.946
Record no names, just one tick on an

00:01:08.107 --> 00:01:09.186
unnamed slip.

00:01:09.467 --> 00:01:11.466
That way no result can be traced back

00:01:11.547 --> 00:01:13.946
to a person, and nobody feels pressured to

00:01:14.027 --> 00:01:14.826
take part.

00:01:16.034 --> 00:01:19.793
Next, write the definitions before anyone counts.

00:01:20.114 --> 00:01:22.353
A free earlobe hangs below the point where

00:01:22.434 --> 00:01:24.233
it joins the side of the head.

00:01:24.514 --> 00:01:27.313
An attached earlobe joins the head directly, with

00:01:27.394 --> 00:01:28.753
no hanging part.

00:01:29.074 --> 00:01:31.313
Some are in between, so add a third

00:01:31.394 --> 00:01:32.833
box: unsure.

00:01:33.714 --> 00:01:36.433
Clear definitions matter, because if two people judge

00:01:36.594 --> 00:01:39.633
the same earlobe differently, the numbers mean different

00:01:39.874 --> 00:01:40.513
things.

00:01:40.594 --> 00:01:42.593
So test the definitions first on a few

00:01:42.994 --> 00:01:46.033
practice diagrams, and if people disagree, sharpen the

00:01:46.194 --> 00:01:46.593
wording.

00:01:47.800 --> 00:01:48.680
Now the method.

00:01:49.240 --> 00:01:52.040
Give each student a slip with three boxes,

00:01:52.120 --> 00:01:53.640
and show the definition card.

00:01:54.280 --> 00:01:56.680
Each student checks their earlobe in a mirror,

00:01:56.800 --> 00:01:59.320
or asks a partner to look, then ticks

00:01:59.400 --> 00:02:01.960
one box, folds the slip and drops it

00:02:02.040 --> 00:02:02.680
in a box.

00:02:03.320 --> 00:02:05.880
Then two people count the slips together onto

00:02:05.920 --> 00:02:08.040
a tally sheet, and check that their totals

00:02:08.120 --> 00:02:08.600
agree.

00:02:09.240 --> 00:02:11.960
Two counters catch mistakes that one person would

00:02:12.160 --> 00:02:12.520
miss.

00:02:13.734 --> 00:02:17.253
Record the counts in a table: category, tally,

00:02:17.334 --> 00:02:18.693
count and frequency.

00:02:19.334 --> 00:02:23.333
Our example class returned thirty slips: nine attached,

00:02:23.414 --> 00:02:25.653
nineteen free and two unsure.

00:02:26.294 --> 00:02:29.093
Frequency is the count divided by the total,

00:02:29.254 --> 00:02:30.053
times one hundred.

00:02:30.614 --> 00:02:31.653
But which total?

00:02:32.214 --> 00:02:34.533
Leave out the unsure slips, since they fit

00:02:34.614 --> 00:02:36.933
neither box, and say so in your table.

00:02:37.494 --> 00:02:39.893
Nine out of twenty-eight is thirty-two per cent

00:02:39.974 --> 00:02:43.333
attached, and nineteen out of twenty-eight is sixty-eight

00:02:43.414 --> 00:02:44.373
per cent free.

00:02:45.600 --> 00:02:48.480
One class is a small sample, so pool

00:02:48.520 --> 00:02:50.240
the results from four classes.

00:02:50.800 --> 00:02:55.280
Their attached frequencies were thirty-two, twenty-two, twenty-eight and

00:02:55.400 --> 00:02:56.560
twenty-seven per cent.

00:02:57.200 --> 00:02:59.920
Altogether, that's thirty attached out of a hundred

00:03:00.000 --> 00:03:02.720
and ten students, about twenty-seven per cent.

00:03:03.440 --> 00:03:05.840
The class values jump around, but the pooled

00:03:05.920 --> 00:03:08.640
value rests on four times as many people,

00:03:08.760 --> 00:03:10.160
so it's a better estimate.

00:03:11.367 --> 00:03:14.086
To present it, use a column graph, because

00:03:14.167 --> 00:03:16.726
the classes are separate groups, not points on

00:03:16.807 --> 00:03:18.246
a continuous scale.

00:03:18.567 --> 00:03:20.726
Put the four classes and the pooled total

00:03:20.847 --> 00:03:23.286
along the bottom, and the frequency of attached

00:03:23.367 --> 00:03:26.246
earlobes, in per cent, up the side, starting

00:03:26.327 --> 00:03:26.966
from zero.

00:03:27.607 --> 00:03:29.926
Leave gaps between the columns, give the graph

00:03:29.967 --> 00:03:33.126
a title, and label both axes, with units

00:03:33.207 --> 00:03:34.646
on the frequency axis.

00:03:35.867 --> 00:03:38.986
You saw earlier that small samples bounce around.

00:03:39.227 --> 00:03:41.146
Now test it yourself with beads.

00:03:41.707 --> 00:03:43.946
Fill a bag with two hundred beads, a

00:03:44.107 --> 00:03:45.786
quarter of them red, so you know the

00:03:45.867 --> 00:03:46.746
true frequency.

00:03:47.387 --> 00:03:50.506
Without looking, draw ten, count the red ones,

00:03:50.587 --> 00:03:51.946
put them back and shake.

00:03:52.587 --> 00:03:56.346
Ten-bead samples gave ten, forty, twenty and thirty

00:03:56.427 --> 00:03:57.706
per cent red.

00:03:57.787 --> 00:04:01.706
Fifty-bead samples gave twenty-two, twenty-eight and twenty-six.

00:04:02.347 --> 00:04:04.986
Small samples scatter widely by chance.

00:04:05.387 --> 00:04:07.786
Bigger samples land closer to the truth.

00:04:09.000 --> 00:04:10.520
Now analyse.

00:04:10.840 --> 00:04:13.120
A trend is the overall direction in the

00:04:13.160 --> 00:04:16.360
data: free earlobes were more common in every

00:04:16.440 --> 00:04:17.000
class.

00:04:17.640 --> 00:04:20.760
A pattern is a feature that repeats: every

00:04:20.840 --> 00:04:23.560
class sat between twenty-two and thirty-two per cent

00:04:23.640 --> 00:04:24.360
attached.

00:04:25.567 --> 00:04:29.326
A relationship is when two characteristics change together,

00:04:29.447 --> 00:04:32.606
so each slip needs both, like earlobes and

00:04:32.687 --> 00:04:33.646
tongue rolling.

00:04:33.967 --> 00:04:36.526
Say twenty of the thirty students with attached

00:04:36.607 --> 00:04:39.886
earlobes could roll their tongue, sixty-seven per cent,

00:04:39.967 --> 00:04:42.446
and fifty-four of the eighty with free earlobes

00:04:42.527 --> 00:04:44.126
could, sixty-eight per cent.

00:04:44.687 --> 00:04:47.486
Almost the same, so this sample shows no

00:04:47.847 --> 00:04:48.366
relationship.

00:04:48.767 --> 00:04:50.766
And even if they had gone together, that

00:04:50.847 --> 00:04:53.566
would be an association, not proof that one

00:04:53.647 --> 00:04:54.686
causes the other.

00:04:55.900 --> 00:04:57.380
Then the limits.

00:04:57.660 --> 00:04:59.820
A hundred and ten students from one school

00:04:59.900 --> 00:05:02.540
is still a small sample, and one school

00:05:02.620 --> 00:05:05.580
may not represent other places, because how common

00:05:05.620 --> 00:05:09.180
a characteristic is can differ between populations.

00:05:09.340 --> 00:05:12.540
People judge their own earlobes differently, and in-between

00:05:12.620 --> 00:05:14.460
ones are hard to call.

00:05:14.620 --> 00:05:17.340
Clear definitions make the data more valid, because

00:05:17.420 --> 00:05:19.900
then it measures what it claims to.

00:05:19.980 --> 00:05:22.860
Two counters, pooling and recounts make it more

00:05:22.940 --> 00:05:25.420
reliable, so repeats agree.

00:05:25.540 --> 00:05:28.380
And always write "in this sample", not "in

00:05:28.540 --> 00:05:29.420
all people".

00:05:30.634 --> 00:05:31.273
Your turn.

00:05:31.834 --> 00:05:35.073
A class samples earlobes in two school groups.

00:05:35.354 --> 00:05:39.113
Group A has eighteen attached and forty-two free.

00:05:39.434 --> 00:05:42.913
Group B has thirty attached and thirty free.

00:05:43.274 --> 00:05:46.073
Calculate the frequency of attached earlobes in each

00:05:46.154 --> 00:05:49.513
group, compare the groups, and give one limitation

00:05:49.554 --> 00:05:50.233
of the data.

00:05:50.794 --> 00:05:52.073
Pause here to answer.

00:06:02.500 --> 00:06:04.340
Each group has sixty people.

00:06:04.820 --> 00:06:07.780
Group A: eighteen out of sixty is thirty

00:06:07.940 --> 00:06:08.820
per cent.

00:06:08.900 --> 00:06:11.780
Group B: thirty out of sixty is fifty

00:06:11.860 --> 00:06:12.820
per cent.

00:06:12.940 --> 00:06:15.540
So attached earlobes were more common in group

00:06:15.620 --> 00:06:17.060
B, in these samples.

00:06:17.620 --> 00:06:20.820
One limitation: sixty people from one school is

00:06:20.900 --> 00:06:23.300
a small sample, so the difference could be

00:06:23.380 --> 00:06:24.420
chance.

00:06:24.900 --> 00:06:27.780
If you divided eighteen by forty-two, you used

00:06:27.860 --> 00:06:28.980
the wrong total.

00:06:30.200 --> 00:06:33.960
So, to collect frequency data well: get agreement

00:06:34.120 --> 00:06:38.040
and keep it anonymous, define the categories first,

00:06:38.120 --> 00:06:41.000
count with two people, divide by the right

00:06:41.080 --> 00:06:44.360
total and say which one, graph it clearly,

00:06:44.440 --> 00:06:45.840
and name the limits.

00:06:46.280 --> 00:06:50.440
Next: how DNA sequencing and profiling show inheritance

00:06:50.680 --> 00:06:52.120
directly in the DNA.

00:06:53.334 --> 00:06:54.773
Here are the key notes.

00:06:55.014 --> 00:06:56.933
Pause the video if you need more time

00:06:57.014 --> 00:06:57.813
to copy them.
